  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "dAcJwXri1qCsiwHng/rN6zxpa9Y=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:28.226Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "This group proposes a deployment rather than an integration: Switchboard running on the partner's own SCS/Kubernetes platform as a reference deployment in their stack. The claim attached to it is a distinction \u2014 sovereignty proven at the record layer, not just the VM layer.\n\nThat distinction is the substance. Sovereign cloud arguments usually stop at where the machines run and who can be compelled to hand them over. They say nothing about whether the business record itself \u2014 the attributed history of what was decided \u2014 is held in a form the operator controls, or whether it lives inside a vendor application that merely happens to run on sovereign infrastructure. Moving the record layer onto the same sovereign platform closes the gap the infrastructure argument leaves open.\n\nThe single lead is GONICUS GmbH, recorded as 80-plus staff in Arnsberg with Bonn and Duesseldorf presence, marked \"2 \u00b7 next\". For a partner whose pitch is already sovereignty, this extends an argument they make rather than introducing one they do not.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- GONICUS GmbH (DE Arnsberg/Bonn/Duesseldorf) \u2014 Stefan Grote + Rainer L\u00fclsdorf + Lars Scheiter \u00b7 info@gonicus.de \u00b7 https://gonicus.de\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> SCS/Kubernetes-hosted Switchboard as a reference deployment in their own stack \u2014 sovereignty proven at the record layer, not just the VM layer. 80+ staff, Arnsberg.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:32:28.226626Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:28.226631Z"
    }
  },
